.TH HD-DASHBOARD 7 "October 2026" "hd-dashboard" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-dashboard \- the hyprdebian dashboard
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-dashboard
turns a hyprdebian system into the hyprdebian dashboard, which shows the state
of every hyprdebian system on one page.
Each system with the package
.B hd-agent
sends a status report to the dashboard every 15 minutes, see
.BR hd-agent (8).
The dashboard also accepts the reports of
.BR hd-report ,
which
.B hd-agent
replaces, from the systems that are not switched to
.B hd-agent
yet.
The systems only make outgoing connections, so no system needs an open port
for it.
.PP
The dashboard keeps the latest report of every system, and a history of the
load, the memory and the fullest pool.
It judges each system on its last report and colors its card by its worst
issue.
.PP
It also relays the jobs of the estate, see
.BR JOBS :
a manager sends the jobs it signed with
.BR hd-manager ,
and the dashboard hands them out to the agents in the replies to their
reports.
It cannot make or change a job, and refuses every job whose signature does not
verify against its
.BR manager_keys .
Its pages show the jobs read-only.
.PP
Installing the package sets everything up:
.IP \(bu 2
the default settings, in the key
.B hd-dashboard
of
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ,
when there are none yet;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR hd-dashboard.service ,
which is enabled and started, and makes the database on its first start.
.PP
The dashboard listens on port 80 by default.
The installation does not open that port in the firewall; that is up to the
admin.
With
.BR ufw (8):
.PP
.RS
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
.RE
.PP
Then the dashboard answers on
.IR http://<host>/ .
.SH THE SERVICE
.B hd-dashboard.service
runs
.BR "hd-dashboard serve" ,
see
.BR hd-dashboard (8),
as a user that systemd makes up, which may bind to port 80 without being root
and may only write
.IR /var/lib/hd-dashboard .
It is enabled on the first installation; after that, it stays as the admin
left it.
An upgrade restarts it when it is enabled.
Only refused reports and jobs, the jobs it stores and errors get a line in
its journal:
.PP
.RS
journalctl \-u hd-dashboard.service
.RE
.PP
.B hd-repo
also uses port 80; give the dashboard another port to have both on one system.
.SH SETTINGS
The settings are the key
.B hd-dashboard
of
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ,
see
.BR hd-config (7).
A setting that is missing has its default value, from
.IR /usr/share/hd-dashboard/defaults.json .
Restart the service after a change:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo hd-config-update hd-dashboard \e
    '{"listen": "0.0.0.0", "port": 8080, "stale_minutes": 45, "offline_days": 14,
      "history_days": 30, "hardening_exceptions": {"beast": ["sysctl"]},
      "manager_keys": ["ssh\-ed25519 AAAA..."]}'
sudo systemctl restart hd-dashboard.service
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B listen
The address to listen on, default
.BR 0.0.0.0 .
.TP
.B port
The port to listen on, default 80.
Allow it in the firewall.
.TP
.B stale_minutes
A system that did not report for this many minutes is offline, which is a
note, default 45.
.TP
.B offline_days
A system that did not report for this many days is an error, default 14, so
a laptop that is off for a while does not turn red.
.TP
.B history_days
How many days the history and the ended jobs are kept, default 30.
.TP
.B hardening_exceptions
The parts of
.BR hd-harden (8)
that are not judged, per host name, from
.BR ssh ,
.BR sysctl ,
.BR modules ,
.B zfs
and
.BR ufw ,
default {}.
.B {"beast": ["sysctl"]}
gives no warning when the sysctls of beast are not hardened.
.TP
.B manager_keys
The public keys of the managers, as
.B hd-manager key
prints them: Ed25519 keys, one per string, default [].
A job must be signed by one of them; without keys every job is refused.
.SH PAGES
.TP
.B POST /report
Stores a report.
The reply has the jobs of the system, see
.BR JOBS .
.TP
.B POST /jobs
Stores signed jobs from a manager, see
.BR JOBS .
.TP
.B GET /
The overview: a card per system, the worst first.
Refreshes every minute.
.TP
.B GET /host/<name>
One system, with the charts of its history, its jobs and its last report.
.TP
.B GET /api/hosts
The systems, their reports, the
.B format
of their last report, their jobs and issues as JSON.
.TP
.B GET /api/jobs
All jobs and their states as JSON.
.TP
.B GET /sequence
The highest sequence number the dashboard has seen, as
.BR {"sequence":\ 412} ;
0 when it has seen none.
.PP
The connection is plain HTTP, meant for the local network.
.SH REPORTS
A system POSTs its report to
.I /report
as a JSON object, at most 256 KiB, with its token in the header
.BR "Authorization: Bearer <token>" .
The token is 16 to 128 letters and digits.
The report has a
.B format
number: 1 from
.BR hd-report ,
2 from
.BR hd-agent ;
reports with a newer format are refused.
.B host
is the host name, by which the dashboard knows the system.
The other parts are
.BR fqdn ,
.BR hyprdebian ,
.BR os ,
.BR load ,
.BR cpus ,
.BR memory ,
.BR pools ,
.BR arrays ,
.BR units ,
.BR updates ,
.BR snapshot ,
.B timers
and
.BR hardening ,
the state of each part that
.BR hd-harden (8)
hardens, which the card shows in green when it is on, in orange when it is
off or changed and in grey when it is unknown or an exception;
the documentation in the repository describes them.
A report of format 2 also has
.BR jobs ,
a list with the state of every job the agent has scheduled and the end of
every job in
.B open
of the last reply that has ended, each with
.B sequence
and
.BR state ,
and for an ended job
.B started
and
.BR ended .
.PP
The first report of a host name registers its token.
Later reports of that host name must have the same token, otherwise they are
refused with 403.
Only a hash of the token is stored.
A reinstalled system has a new token: forget the old one with
.BR "hd-dashboard forget" ,
and the next report registers the new one.
.SH JOBS
A job is one command for one system, to run once, not before
.B not_before
and not after
.BR expires ,
signed by a manager.
The dashboard stores it as it was sent, with its state, its start and end
time and when it was stored.
.PP
A manager POSTs a JSON array of signed jobs to
.IR /jobs .
The dashboard stores them all, as waiting, or none.
It refuses them with 400 when one is malformed, 403 when the signature of one
does not verify against any of
.BR manager_keys ,
422 when one is for a host it has no token for, and 409 when the sequence
number of one is taken: not above the highest it has seen.
It counts as seen the sequence numbers of the jobs it stores and those in the
reports, and keeps the highest when the jobs are removed.
.PP
The reply to a report is a JSON object with
.BR jobs ,
the waiting jobs of the system in order of sequence, as they were signed, and
.BR open ,
the sequence numbers of its jobs that have not ended.
A job is handed out with every reply until a report shows it as scheduled or
ended.
A waiting job whose expiry has passed is marked expired by the dashboard.
A report only changes the jobs of its own system, and not one that ended ok,
failed or cancelled.
Ended jobs are removed
.B history_days
after their end.
.SH ISSUES
Errors: no report for longer than
.BR offline_days ,
failed units, a pool that is not ONLINE or 90% full or more, errors found by
the last completed scrub of a pool, a degraded mdadm
array, a failed last run of the service of a timer, and Secure Boot that is
not on or not reported, see
.BR hd-secboot (8),
and a failed job, until a newer job with the same command ends ok.
.PP
Warnings: an expired job, until a newer job with the same command ends ok,
a pool that is 80% full or more, bytes repaired by the last
completed scrub of a pool, a system that is starting or
stopping, a part of
.BR hd-harden (8)
that is off or changed and not in
.BR hardening_exceptions ,
or no hardening in the report, a pending reboot, no upgrade for 14 days or never, and a HyprDebian
version older than the one on the system of the dashboard, from
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/version.json .
A newer version gets no warning; without that file, versions are not judged.
.PP
Notes: a system that did not report for longer than
.BR stale_minutes ,
a part of hd-harden whose state is unknown, and upgradable packages.
Notes do not change the color of the card.
.SH REMOVAL
Removing the package stops the service and keeps the settings and the
database.
Purging it also removes the key
.BR hd-dashboard
and the database.
Firewall rules for the dashboard stay; remove them by hand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/hd-dashboard
The dashboard.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/hd-dashboard.service
Runs the dashboard.
.TP
.I /usr/share/hd-dashboard/defaults.json
The default settings.
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/config.json
The settings, in the key
.BR hd-dashboard .
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/version.json
The version of HyprDebian on this system, installed by hd-base.
Hosts with an older version get a warning.
.TP
.I /var/lib/hd-dashboard/dashboard.db
The SQLite database, kept by systemd in
.IR /var/lib/private/hd-dashboard .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-dashboard (8),
.BR hd-agent (8),
.BR hd-secboot (8),
.BR hd-config (7),
.BR hd-config-update (1),
.BR ufw (8),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
